1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,952
If a language model had no KV cache, how would you design a coding agent?

2
00:00:07,102 --> 00:00:17,140
Reusing a prefix is cheap. Change one early token and everything after is reprocessed.

3
00:00:17,290 --> 00:00:22,440
Take the cache away: six familiar flaws, one cause

4
00:00:23,340 --> 00:00:35,946
Six symptoms: routing · tools · compaction · sub-agents · restarts · batteries

5
00:00:36,096 --> 00:00:42,998
None of the six is a model capability gap. It is what the harness feeds the model.

6
00:00:43,898 --> 00:00:52,424
Pure Opus: 25·Y + 5·Z

7
00:00:52,574 --> 00:01:01,028
Opus → Sonnet → Opus: 3X + 20Y + 8Z

8
00:01:01,178 --> 00:01:12,496
X=0.65 Y=0.12 Z=0.23 → 4.15 against 6.19

9
00:01:13,396 --> 00:01:22,546
Reading files 30–40% · search 10–18% · command output 10–20%

10
00:01:22,696 --> 00:01:29,686
Writing code 4–10%: the reason the agent exists, the smallest line item

11
00:01:29,836 --> 00:01:43,338
fastcontext: reading and searching, 56.2% of tool turns, 46.5% of tokens

12
00:01:44,238 --> 00:01:53,340
The same grep output: twelve hits for this query, hidden for the next

13
00:01:53,490 --> 00:02:02,352
Four levels: hidden / short / long / full

14
00:02:02,502 --> 00:02:07,508
The model does not have to get better. What it sees does.
